description: When writing code or tests that must parse Excel addresses that might include ranges
alwaysApply: false
---
## Safe Range Parsing Rules

- **Normalize first, parse second.**  
  Always run formulas through `FormulaNormalizer.normalize(...)` before extracting refs, so `A1:A3`, `Sheet1!A1:A3`, and `Sheet1!A1:Sheet1!A3` converge to one canonical form (`Sheet1!A1:A3` — single sheet prefix for same-sheet ranges). Cross-sheet ranges keep both endpoints qualified (`Sheet1!A1:Sheet2!D1`).

- **Use parser utilities, never ad-hoc colon splitting for formulas.**  
  For formula text:
  - `parse_range_refs_with_spans(...)` returns (`start_ref`, `end_ref`, `span`), where `span` is the matched range's character offsets in the original formula as (`start`, `end`) (end-exclusive).
  - Prefer `parse_standalone_cell_refs(...)` (or `parse_standalone_cell_refs_with_spans`) when combining range parsing with single-cell parsing so bare endpoints in single-prefix ranges are not double-counted or mis-sheeted. `parse_cell_refs` refuses formula text that still contains range spans unless `allow_unmasked_ranges=True`.
  - `parse_range_refs(...)` returns (`start_ref`, `end_ref`) when spans are unnecessary.
  - `expand_range(...)` enumerates cells within a range.

- **Keep expansion limits consistent across call paths.**  
  If one path uses `max_range_cells`, all related paths (builder, provenance, dynamic-ref arg env) must use the same cap to avoid mismatched dependency/candidate sets.

- **Treat sheet-qualified endpoints as first-class.**  
  Accept all equivalent forms, including quoted sheets:  
  `A1:A3`, `Sheet1!A1:A3`, `Sheet1!A1:Sheet1!A3`, `'My Sheet'!A1:'My Sheet'!A3`.

- **For `INDIRECT`/text-to-range parsing, use shared address parsing helpers.**  
  Don’t hand-roll tuple ordering or endpoint-sheet checks in each module; route through standardized helpers so row/col orientation and same-sheet validation stay consistent.

## Anti-Patterns (Do Not Do)

- `split(":")`, `partition(":")`, `if ":" in ...` for **formula range semantics**
- custom regex-only range extraction in business logic when parser utilities already exist
- separate “mini parsers” that drift from normalizer/parser behavior

## Minimum Regression Test Matrix

For each consumer that handles ranges (dependency extraction, dynamic refs, INDIRECT/text parsing), include tests for:

- `A1:A3`
- `Sheet1!A1:A3`
- `Sheet1!A1:Sheet1!A3`
- `'My Sheet'!A1:'My Sheet'!A3`
- interior-cell inclusion after expansion (not just endpoints)

## Issue #151 audit (manual `:` split inventory)

Not every colon split belongs in `parse_range_refs*`—some paths parse **address strings**, not formula text.

- **Formula dependency extraction:** `parse_range_refs*` + `expand_range` + normalizer (builder, provenance).
- **`INDIRECT` text / sheet-qualified address strings:** `split_sheet_qualified_address` or `indirect_text_to_range` (`excel_grapher.core.addressing`); named-range resolver INDIRECT shares this.
- **Exported `xl_range(...)` runtime addresses:** `runtime/cache.py` uses `split_sheet_qualified_address` so quoted-sheet escaping matches other paths.
- **Config / CLI specs (non-formula):** `blank_ranges.py`, codegen constant-range parsing — sheet+`cell_part` split after `!`; acceptable when input is not normalized formula text.
- **Dynamic-ref constraint addresses:** `dynamic_refs._expand_sheet_qualified_range` — splits local `A1:A3` after sheet is fixed; cell expansion uses `expand_range`.
- **Examples / one-off scripts:** ad-hoc splits OK if outside evaluator/export parity surface.
